247 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 146 0 R
/E (More importantly, the restricted mobility can have broader and more significant implications for higher education and career trajectories. Students that relocate for higher education - domestically or abroad - often depend on outside food due to time constraints. A similar pattern can be seen in people that migrate for careers. However, severe food allergies - more specifically, the risk of cross-contamination cannot be accommodated by most facilities that produce food on a mass-scale. Moreover, eateries that are able to cater to such concerns tend to be priced such that they may not be a viable option in the long-term for a daily basis. Once again, there is a strong case for privilege mitigating the impact of disability; by being able to spend on safe outside food instead of cooking themselves, people are able to perform better at work or studies. Clearly, the incidence of food allergies \(as a disability\) is borne by those affected: be it in terms of higher prices for safer food \(if they could afford it\) or the time that they would have to devote to cooking instead of studying or being at work. There are limited measures whatsoever to redistribute the incidence/ lessen the burden of food allergies in this regard. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
