/E (The inclusion of people who are different than us in our daily lives has now become a routine. Prayer rooms in offices, availability of food items based on different preferences, adjustable podiums, chairs for people with all heights are some of the examples. Although they are quite simple, we do not term these accommodations as burdens. If anything, they are the logistical decisions that help diverse people to participate without being evaluated based on the competence or worth of the participants. In these cases, support is seen as design and not charity. )
