296 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 153 0 R
/T (The shift that is required to make disability inclusive is fundamentally attitudinal. Instead of perceiving disability as a defining constraint, it must be viewed as a component of human diversity. Assumption of voluntarism should be practiced instead of assumption of reliance. Resources helping to satisfy our expectations should be provided instead of decreasing them. Making someone feel welcome instead of different is what inclusivity aims for. It is about ensuring that diversity or difference does not result in respect that is lessened or diminished.  )
/E (The shift that is required to make disability inclusive is fundamentally attitudinal. Instead of perceiving disability as a defining constraint, it must be viewed as a component of human diversity. Assumption of voluntarism should be practiced instead of assumption of reliance. Resources helping to satisfy our expectations should be provided instead of decreasing them. Making someone feel welcome instead of different is what inclusivity aims for. It is about ensuring that diversity or difference does not result in respect that is lessened or diminished.  )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
297 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 153 0 R
/T (The goal of inclusivity is not to erase the differences or pretending that the disability does not exist or it does not matter. It is nor the romanticising of struggle. It is simply to demolish the hierarchy that tends to rank the differences as inferior. Accommodation should not threaten the dignity thereby triggering discomfort. A ramp for wheelchair should not be treated as a favour, or extra time in exams should not be perceived as an advantage since they are mere mechanisms that help societal institutions correct their own narrow designs. Without dismantling this hierarchy, inclusion risks becoming symbolic that is present in policy but absent in experience.  )
/E (The goal of inclusivity is not to erase the differences or pretending that the disability does not exist or it does not matter. It is nor the romanticising of struggle. It is simply to demolish the hierarchy that tends to rank the differences as inferior. Accommodation should not threaten the dignity thereby triggering discomfort. A ramp for wheelchair should not be treated as a favour, or extra time in exams should not be perceived as an advantage since they are mere mechanisms that help societal institutions correct their own narrow designs. Without dismantling this hierarchy, inclusion risks becoming symbolic that is present in policy but absent in experience.  )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
298 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 153 0 R
/T (Ultimately, if individuals with disabilities need to feel included in its truest sense, they must experience equality both in perception and access. They must be directly addressed, sincerely evaluated and supported stigma-free. Their wants and needs must be met without being exaggerated into the indicators of otherness.     )
/E (Ultimately, if individuals with disabilities need to feel included in its truest sense, they must experience equality both in perception and access. They must be directly addressed, sincerely evaluated and supported stigma-free. Their wants and needs must be met without being exaggerated into the indicators of otherness.     )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [5]
>>
