/P 159 0 R
/E (We use words like "special needs" or "accommodation," which sound helpful, but they actually shift the blame. When a lecture hall doesn't have a ramp, we don't say the building is broken; we say the wheelchair user "requires an accommodation." If an office doesn't use accessible software, we don't say the workplace is narrow-minded; we say the employee "needs extra support." By framing it this way, we make the person the "problem" to be solved, rather than the environment. )
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [4]
>>
